# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this file, line-ending behaviour is whatever each developer's
# core.autocrlf happens to be. This repository is developed on Windows
# (autocrlf=true) and built on Linux CI, so a .sql or .cmd file committed with
# CRLF from Windows can break the Linux runners that execute it — and a shell
# script with CRLF fails with an unhelpful "\r: command not found".
#
# The rule is: the repository stores LF; the working tree gets native endings.
# That is the one arrangement where a Windows and a Linux developer can both
# commit without producing a whole-file diff.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files that MUST stay LF even on Windows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything a shell or an interpreter reads line-by-line. These are the files
# where CRLF is a correctness problem rather than a diff-noise problem.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files that MUST stay CRLF on Windows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A .cmd batch file with LF works, but a .bat launched from a shortcut in some
# Windows configurations is happier with native endings, and cmd.exe is the one
# interpreter where the convention is worth matching.

*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary: never touched by any eol conversion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this, `* text=auto` makes git guess at every PNG, ICO and WOFF, and a
# wrong guess silently corrupts the file.

*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.avif      binary
*.ico       binary
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.eot       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.mp3       binary
*.mp4       binary
*.webm      binary
*.mov       binary
*.wav       binary
*.ogg       binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated and vendored: stored as-is, never normalised.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# package-lock.json is generated by npm and already LF; a conversion here would
# produce a spurious diff against what `npm ci` expects.

package-lock.json      text eol=lf
**/package-lock.json   text eol=lf
go.sum                 text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Diffs and merges.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A migration is a sequence of line-final semicolons with dollar-quoted bodies.
# A merge conflict marker spliced into one of those is not always obvious, so
# conflict markers are made visible in the diff rather than hidden by a
# heuristic that only looks for whole-line markers.

*.sql   diff=sql
*.go    diff=golang
*.ts    diff=typescript
*.tsx   diff=typescript

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language statistics.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this, .sql is attributed to "Others" and .tsx is counted as a separate
# language from .ts, which distorts the repository profile for no benefit.

*.sql    linguist-language=SQL
*.tsx    linguist-language=TypeScript
*.md     linguist-documentation=true
docs/**  linguist-documentation=true
